\f0\b IN THE BOX
\f1\b0 \
\
- Bare AntiHunter DIGI NODE PCB\
\
\

\f0\b SOURCE THE PARTS
\f1\b0 \
\
Full bill of materials with part links and photographs:\
\

\f0\b github.com/lukeswitz/AntiHunter \'96 hw folder - BOM-Links.md
\f1\b0 \
\
Core: XIAO ESP32-S3, Heltec V3.2 LoRa radio or T114, ATGM336H GPS, DS3231 RTC, SW-420 vibration sensor, micro SD reader, SD card in FAT32 up to 16 GB, KSD9700 thermal switch at 40 C, waterproof Type-C female chassis jack for the external 5 V input.\
\
\

\f0\b BUILD IT
\f1\b0 \
\
1. Work through the assembly manual end to end:\
\

\f0\b github.com/lukeswitz/AntiHunter \'96 hw folder, Antihunter-DIGINODE-AssemblyManual.pdf
\f1\b0 \
\
2. Two modules need a header modification first, covered on page 6: the RTC ships with female headers, and some tamper sensors ship with inverted 90 degree headers.\
\
3. Fit the SD card before you close the case. The slot is not reachable once the PCB is mounted.\
\
\

\f0\b FLASH IT
\f1\b0 \
\
4. Flash the Heltec V3.2 or T114 with the latest stable Meshtastic from flasher.meshtastic.org, then set the serial module: Enabled, mode TEXTMSG, baud 115200, pins 19 RX / 20 TX on Heltec V3 or 10 RX / 9 TX on T114. Without this the node and the radio cannot talk.\
\
5. Flash AntiHunter on the ESP32 over USB. Web flasher: lukeswitz.github.io/AntiHunter. Pick Full for the web UI, or Headless for quiet field ops.\
\
6. Set the LoRa region, change the Bluetooth pairing pin, and make your own encrypted channel primary with the public default off.\
\
7. Full firmware: join the 
\f0\b Antihunter
\f1\b0  WiFi AP, password 
\f0\b antihunt3r123
\f1\b0 , open http://192.168.4.1 and change those credentials first.\
\
\

\f0\b POWER
\f1\b0 \
\
Regulated 5 V only, from USB, a bench supply set to 5 V, a proper DC-DC regulator or a UPS board. Never wire 18650 cells or an unregulated supply to the PCB. Never power the node with the LoRa antenna missing. One USB cable at a time: the ESP32 and the radio share the power rails.\
\
\

